592	## Precision and bit depth
593	
594	- **16-bit float compositing.** Every frame is composited at 16-bit float, and every video source is decoded at full depth. Gradients from a 10-bit camera file survive grading.
595	- **10-bit exports.** HEVC 10-bit (Main 10), both AV1 formats, ProRes 422 and ProRes 4444 keep that depth. H.264, HEVC and HEVC with Alpha are 8-bit.
596	- **Deep stills.** A still deeper than 8 bits per channel, such as a 16-bit PNG or TIFF or a 10-bit HEIC, is drawn at 16-bit float. An 8-bit still is drawn as before.
597	- **Saved and rendered frames.** **File ▸ Save Frame…** writes a 16-bit PNG or a 10-bit HEIC when the frame shows a deep still or deep video, and in every HDR (HLG) project. JPEG, Animated GIF and Animated PNG are always 8-bit.
598	- **Reversed clips.** A reversed clip's backwards copy is rendered at 10 bits, at the source's exact size, with the source's own colour labels. That means an HDR or log clip still reads correctly when reversed. See [Speed](speed.md).
599	- **What stays 8-bit.** Text, shapes, captions, graphics, thumbnails and screen recordings are 8-bit. In an HDR (HLG) project they are still lifted to HDR reference white.
600	
601	## Limits
602	|---|---|---|
603	| Encoded | Layers blend on their encoded values, as most editors do | Yes |
604	| Linear light | Layers blend as light. A dissolve between two saturated clips keeps its brightness instead of dipping dark at the midpoint. A title fading over video and a blurred or feathered edge come out brighter, and a glow adds light the way light does. | |
605	
606	The change is one undo step, **Blending**. It changes no clip and no grade. Grades, the chroma key, qualifiers, Show Selection, luma mattes and the scopes still read the same values they did, so a grade looks the same either way. In an HDR (HLG) project, "linear" means HLG's scene light.
607	
608	*Agents:* `set_canvas` with `blending` set to `"encoded"` or `"linear"`.
609	
610	## Exporting HDR
611	
612	In an HDR (HLG) project, the export sheet gets a **Dynamic Range:** row under **Format**. It decides what the 10-bit formats write:
613	
614	| Item | What the file holds | Default |
615	|---|---|---|
616	| HDR (HLG) | The BT.2100 HLG master, Dolby Vision off | Yes |
617	| HDR10 (PQ) | BT.2100 PQ, mastered at 1,000 cd/m² | |
618	| SDR version | The SDR version, Rec. 709 | |
619	
620	- The row is live only on **HEVC 10-bit (MP4)**, **AV1 (MP4)**, **AV1 (WebM)**, **VP9 (WebM)**, **ProRes 422 (MOV)** and **ProRes 4444 (MOV)**. On every other video format it is disabled and shows **SDR version**, because those formats always write the SDR version: H.264, HEVC, HEVC with Alpha, VP9 with Alpha (WebM), Animated GIF and Animated PNG.
621	- **VP9 (WebM)** writes HLG and HDR10 as 10-bit VP9 Profile 2, and the SDR version as 8-bit Profile 0. VP9 itself cannot say which transfer it uses, so the WebM file's colour information is what tells a player it is HLG or PQ.
622	- An HDR10 file carries fixed HDR10 metadata: a P3 D65 mastering display of 0.005–1,000 cd/m², MaxCLL 1,000 and MaxFALL 0. Its picture never goes above 1,000 cd/m², so the metadata is always accurate. The Media tab's HDR10 Peak describes a *source* file and does not change this.
623	- The choice applies to that one sheet only. It is not remembered for the next export or saved in an export preset.
624	- An SDR project's export sheet has no Dynamic Range row.
625	
626	For formats, sizes and the export queue, see [Export](export.md).
627	
628	*Agents:* `export` with `dynamic_range` set to `"hlg"`, `"pq"` or `"sdr"`. `hlg` and `pq` are refused on an 8-bit preset ("… is 8-bit and writes the SDR version …") and in an SDR project ("… needs an HLG project; set_canvas output \"hlg\" first"). `export` answers an `export_id` at once; once `export_status` reports the job `done`, its `dynamic_range` names the picture that was written (`hlg`, `pq` or `sdr`).
